.\"
.\" MOUNT(2) — Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH MOUNT 2 "May 2026" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
mount \- attach a filesystem to the namespace
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RB ( libc ", " -lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/mount.h>
.PP
.BI "int mount(const char *" source ", const char *" target ,
.BI "          const char *" filesystemtype ", unsigned long " mountflags ,
.BI "          const void *" data );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mount ()
attaches the filesystem named by
.I filesystemtype
on
.I source
to the directory
.IR target .
For network or virtual filesystems
.I source
may be NULL.
.PP
.I source
may also be given as
.BI LABEL= name
(as on Linux): the kernel scans every block device, reads its on-disk
volume label, and mounts the one whose label equals
.IR name .
This fails with
.B ENODEV
if no device carries that label.  Volume-label reading is provided by the
filesystem driver: ext2
.RI ( s_volume_name ),
FAT
.RI ( "root-dir volume-label entry, else BS_VolLab" ),
and UDF
.RI ( "LVD logical volume identifier" ).
Filesystems with no label concept are skipped.
.PP
.I mountflags
is a bitmask of:
.TP
.B MNT_RDONLY
Mount read-only.
.TP
.B MNT_NOSUID
Ignore set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits when executing files on this fs.
.TP
.B MNT_NODEV
Disallow access to special files.
.TP
.B MNT_NOEXEC
Disallow program execution.
.TP
.B MNT_SYNCHRONOUS
Force synchronous writes.
.TP
.B MNT_ASYNC
Defer all writes (best-effort).
.TP
.B MNT_UPDATE
Re-mount an already-mounted filesystem to change its flags rather
than overlaying a new one.
.PP
The
.I data
argument is filesystem-specific.  Most filesystems accept a
NUL-terminated comma-separated options string of
.B key=value
pairs (parsed by the kernel's mountopt subsystem); the framework
strips the standard options
.RB ( ro ", " rw ", " nosuid ", " nodev ", " noexec ", " sync ", " async )
out of that string and folds them into
.I mountflags
before handing the remainder to the filesystem driver.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.  On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller's effective UID is not 0.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I mountflags
contains conflicting bits, or
.I target
is malformed.
.TP
.B ENOENT
.I target
does not exist.
.TP
.B ENOTDIR
.I target
is not a directory.
.TP
.B EBUSY
.I target
is one of the kernel-managed paths
.RB ( /dev ", " /proc ", " /sys )
and
.B MNT_UPDATE
was not specified, or the filesystem couldn't be locked.
.TP
.B EUNKNOWNFS
.I filesystemtype
isn't registered.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.IR source ", " target ", or " filesystemtype
points outside the calling process's address space.
.TP
.B EIO
The filesystem driver's mount handler failed.
.SH SECURITY
.BR mount ()
requires effective UID 0.  Mount-over-critical-path is rejected
unless
.B MNT_UPDATE
is set; this guards against shadowing
.B /dev
or
.B /proc
with attacker-controlled content.  The kernel logs every failed mount
attempt with
.BR kprintf .
.SH NOTES
.SS Substrate Native ABI
.nf
mount:    syscall number 21
umount:   syscall number 22
umount2:  syscall number 161
.fi
.SS Supported Filesystems
ext2, fat, exfat, minix, udf, devfs, procfs, sysfs, fuse, 9p.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umount (2),
.BR statvfs (2),
.BR statfs (2),
.BR mount (8)
